 "deepDive": {
  "domain": "Law, courts & regulation",
  "title": "Tribunals, asylum and juvenile age: the week in law",
  "body": "Monday's legal picture has one common thread: courts and tribunals being asked to settle questions politics has not.\n\n**Poland.** The fight over the Constitutional Tribunal comes to a head this week. Judge Korwin-Piotrowska, acting as the Tribunal's head, has called a General Assembly for **Nov 17** to decide on lifting the immunity of suspended president Święczkowski, including consent to detain him, and of judge Sochański. The President's office disputes her authority and warns of two rival 'Tribunals'. President Nawrocki's chief of staff hosts all-party talks at the Belweder today, which KO and Lewica are boycotting. Separately, Nawrocki has until about **Nov 27** to sign, veto or refer the government's 'rule of law' act, which voids resolutions of the post-2018 KRS and reorganises the status of judges it appointed. A veto is widely expected ([Rzeczpospolita](https://www.rp.pl/sady-i-trybunaly/art45457341-17-listopada-tk-zbierze-sie-zeby-zdecydowac-o-losie-bogdana-swieczkowskiego); [Interia](https://wydarzenia.interia.pl/kraj/news-senat-zdecydowal-ws-ustawy-praworzadnosciowej-chodzi-o-tzw-n,nId,23570104)).\n\n**EU in Poland.** The new consumer-credit directive (CCD2) applies from **Nov 20**, bringing buy-now-pay-later and small loans under full credit rules. Poland has not passed its implementing law, which leaves lenders and borrowers unsure which rules apply.\n\n**US.** On Nov 13 the Supreme Court agreed to review Trump's 2025 proclamation denying asylum at the southern border, which lower courts held exceeded presidential power. A ruling is likely by June 2027 ([Bloomberg](https://www.bloomberg.com/news/articles/2026-11-13/supreme-court-to-weigh-keeping-trump-southern-border-asylum-ban)). In Washington, Judge Ana Reyes blocked the East Potomac golf-course closure until Nov 28 and plans a special master to examine whether officials misled the court.\n\n**India.** The Kerala High Court urged legislators to consider lowering the age for juveniles in conflict with the law from 18 to 16. The remark is not binding, but it feeds a live national debate; the 2015 JJ Act already lets 16–17-year-olds accused of heinous crimes be tried as adults ([ThePrint](https://theprint.in/india/kerala-hc-suggests-lowering-juvenile-age-to-16-cites-growing-maturity-of-children/3098853/)).",
